From a2734255f94a526d28bd2c0702eb049980f636da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 08:10:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] The passes keep their history across the presented frames

Patch 0008 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled, .scaffold/upgrades/lighting-design.md).

Every pass is one noisy sample per pixel per frame. The direct pass's factor and the scatter are
blended now with what the previous presented frame held for the same surface point: the point is
projected through the previous frame's view projection, and the history there is kept only where
the previous depth and normal say it was the same surface (the depth compared as the point's own
distance from the previous eye, in the depth view's encoding). The renderer interpolates the
camera on the CPU for every presented frame, so the previous frame's view is the previous
presented one and the traced passes run once per presented frame with the interpolated camera;
nothing is traced once per game update and reprojected in between, because the passes cost a few
milliseconds at 1440p and every presented frame is a new sample.

A cut clears the history: the eye moving more than 150 units or the view turning more than about
25 degrees between two presented frames, a resize, the first frames. The parameter block carries
the previous eye and the flag. The direct pass counts, on one pixel in sixty four, how many took
their history and how many were traced, into a small buffer copied to a readback slot per ring
position, and the trace prints `[rt] reprojected n of m` once a second.

Pinned edits, each marked "patch 0008": the previous eye and the flag in the parameter block; the
scatter texture's pair and the count buffer in the common set and the register block; the
bindings, barriers and the count's clear and copy around the direct dispatch; the previous eye and
the cut test where the previous view projection is computed.

